      "body": "A Job Digest's page is its conversation with your AI coach: it has the new job offers found for the profile and their Match Scores in view. Until you have written anything, suggestions wait above the message box: « Which offers to pick? », « Refine my criteria ». A Job Digest never counts against a quota.\n\nOn the right, the Job Digest's fiche: its profile, the day it was sent and how many job offers it holds, then, while job offers wait for you, a line saying how many you have left to handle, which leads to « Offers to handle », in Job searches. The Job Digest's email ends with the same line, under its button. The panel's « Job offers found » view shows each job offer with its Match Score; in the fiche, the « Job offers found » section lists them all and « Settings » opens how often this profile's Job Digest comes, which you change in place, with the latest ones sent.\n\nThe activity shows the page's history: the Job Digest, its Match Scores computed with « No AI », the offers it brought, each « ↳ follows » it, and the coach's replies in this conversation.\n\nA job offer or the settings open beside the conversation, without hiding it: save a job offer to follow it in your applications. « Close » or the Escape key closes them.\n\nOn a phone, the conversation takes the whole screen. « Fiche » opens the panel over it, full screen, the other views are under « … », and a job offer opens full screen too: « Close » or « Close the panel » closes them and brings you back to the conversation.\n\nIn the Jobbbox app (iPhone, iPad, Android), a Job Digest's page shows its profile, the day it was sent and its job offers, closest to your master CV first, above its conversation. Tap a job offer to open it: its Match Score against the profile, a button explaining the score detailing each criterion, its summary and its text. « Follow » creates its application with this profile; once followed, « See the application » opens it. « Set aside » takes it out of your « Offers to handle », with a reason if you wish, and « Undo » goes back on it; a job offer set aside keeps « Go back on this choice »."
